.TH CHTTPSVR_USE 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_use \- append global middleware to the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_use(chttpsvr srv,
                            chttpsvr_middleware_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends a middleware function to the global middleware chain.  For
every request, a global middleware runs before the middleware of each
router and before the route handler.

fn receives a next callback; calling next continues the chain.  A
middleware that never calls next stops the chain there, and the server
sends the response as it stands.

A global middleware also runs for a request that the server rejects
before a handler can run, so a rate limiter, a per-peer ban list or an
access log written as a middleware sees that traffic too.  Three
rejections run the chain:
  - A 404, when no route matched the path.
  - A 405, when no route matched the method.
  - A 413, when the declared Content-Length is above max_body_size.
For a 404 or a 405 of a path under a mounted prefix, the chain is the
global chain plus the chain of the sub-router that owns the path (see
chttpsvr_subrouter(3)); for a 404 or a 405 of any other path, it is
the global chain only.  Because the server decides a 413 after a route
has matched, a 413 runs the full effective chain of that request,
including the sub-router middleware.

No handler is reached in these cases, so a chain that runs to its end
sends the rejection response as it stands, while a middleware that
does not call next answers the request itself, exactly as it would
for a matched route.  The request has a method, a path, headers and a
query string to read, but no path parameters, because no pattern
captured any, and the server never reads its body, so
chttpsvr_req_param returns NULL and chttpsvr_req_read returns -1.  In
both cases the server closes the connection after the response.

A response that a middleware produces for such a request is sent as
an internally generated rejection, so the small unconditional ceiling
on the write duration, described under
max_response_write_duration_us in CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3), applies
to it.  Keep such a response small, as a rejection response would
be.

Three rejections run no middleware, by design:
  - A 500 comes from an allocation failure in the server, and
    application code that allocates more memory is the wrong answer
    to memory pressure.
  - A 503 means that the worker pool is full, so more application
    work must not start.
  - The server decides a 501 or a 505 before it routes the request:
    a 501 for a method token outside the seven chttp_method_t names
    or for a transfer coding that it does not implement, and a 505
    for an HTTP version whose major number is not 1.  There is
    therefore no routed request for a middleware to see.
The server also sends a rejection without middleware in two further
cases:
  - Its rejection thread pool is full or shutting down, and the
    server must write the response inline; this limits how much
    application code a flood of rejected requests can start.
  - The server cannot allocate the decoded path of the request,
    because the server guarantees every middleware a readable
    chttpsvr_req_path(3).

This function is thread-safe and can be called both before and after
the server starts.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Each router, both the root router (which holds the global middleware)
and each sub-router, caps its own middleware chain at 32 entries.
This cap is applied at REGISTRATION time: chttpsvr_use and
chttpsvr_router_use return ccol_not_permitted, and add no entry, on
the call that would take that one router above the cap of 32 entries.

At dispatch time a separate combined cap of 32 applies to the
effective chain of one request, which is the global middleware plus
the middleware of the router that matched.  Because each side of that
sum can hold up to 32 entries on its own, the combined count can
exceed 32 even though neither router reached its own cap at
registration time; the server then answers the affected request with
HTTP 500.

You can register the same path and method more than once, and each
registration returns ccol_success, but under a first-one-wins policy
the server only ever calls the FIRST handler that you registered.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID, when srv is a stale
                  handle that has already been destroyed, or when fn
                  is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
ccol_not_permitted when the middleware chain of the root router is
already at the cap of 32 entries.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_router_use (3)
